`,e.jsxs(r.p,{children:["The Pelican recording captures the game canvas at 1280 × 720 through ",e.jsx(r.code,{children:"canvas.captureStream()"}),". GameMap used normal W-key cycling, the game's built-in cinematic camera, and its U-key HUD toggle. The resulting browser recording is credited to GameMap as a capture of the author's game; the camera behavior, scene, and animation are supplied by that game."]}),`
`,e.jsxs(r.p,{children:["The Code World Model chapter shows generated visual observations from an executable-state framework: a coding agent updates persistent world state, code compiles it into proxy controls, and MiniMax-H3 renders the view. The author's ",e.jsx(r.a,{href:"https://huggingface.co/datasets/NTU-yiwen/code-world-model-project-page-videos/resolve/main/gallery_final/proxy/002.mp4",children:"matching proxy video 002"})," preserves the state-to-observation comparison. This excerpt is labeled a research demo. The ",e.jsx(r.a,{href:"https://github.com/buaacyw/code-world-model/blob/main/ENVIRONMENT.md",children:"public inference documentation"})," states that the closed-source game foundation and tools generating proxy inputs cannot be redistributed; the recording does not establish a complete publicly playable game or a fully released agent-to-game pipeline."]}),`
`,e.jsx(r.p,{children:"Credit remains with OpenGame / CUHK MMLab, Pelican creator riba2534, and Code World Model authors Yiwen Chen, Guosheng Lin, and Chi Zhang. GameMap supplies the Pelican capture, selection, assembly, and branding. Underlying games, characters, artwork, research outputs, and author recordings retain their respective rights holders. The official Code World Model video dataset identifies its files as public research-demo assets but supplies no explicit video redistribution license. Repository code licenses do not automatically license these recordings, generated observations, or third-party characters."}),`
